  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nWith the high pressure now moving into the Great Lakes our flow\nis shifting to the south which will increase WAA. This will \nstart a gradual warming trend with highs up to around 80 today \nand the lower to mid 80s tomorrow. This is a notable increase \nfrom the lower to mid 70s we hit yesterday. Friday will start \nto see more moisture advection as well. This will help to \ndevelop a more unstable environment, but this is more focused\nacross central Minnesota and north. Farther south will see see \nwarmer mid levels which will limit instability. Friday will also\nsee a shortwave move across the Canadian Prairies and with this\npath the main lift will be mostly to our north across North \nDakota into northern Minnesota. Parts of central Minnesota could\nsee the southern extent of these storms, but instability runs \nout quickly farther south.\n\nSaturday will be the first of the hot days as a ridge builds in.\nThis ridge looks to be the dominant pattern of the rest of the\nweek. So the heat looks to return and stay for a while, which\nincreasing the impact of heat stress. Much above normal \ntemperatures look to be a lock for Sunday into early next week \nwith 90 plus percent of the global ensemble members with high \ntemperatures above normal. So the main forecast questions come\ndown to how much above normal and how widespread will the heat \nbe? So while above normal is high confidence, how high is where\nsome spread still exists. A \"cool\" solution would see highs \nmore in the 5 to 10 degrees above normal with the hot solutions\npushing well above normal in the upper 90s to a few members \npushing over 100. On the rain/storms side our second chance \ncomes late Sunday into early Monday morning. The better signal \nin both global deterministic and ensemble solutions remains to \nour north and east. So we would once again we along the edge of \nthe chances. Looking into next week, QPF in the ensembles get \nwidely scattered with no clear precipitation signal. It is not a\ndry result so we could see something come together as we get\ncloser.",
